It bundles and builds upon third-party material from MT-JailBench
("MT-JailBench: A benchmark framework for studying multi-turn jailbreak
attacks", SafetyArena, arXiv:2605.11002), pinned at commit
cb8184e0a9ad7a65c86a00e2a38c57a5f58043f0, and from HarmBench (the behavior
dataset MT-JailBench redistributes). Upstream license texts are preserved
verbatim in LICENSES/mt-jailbench-MIT.txt and LICENSES/harmbench-MIT.txt.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Judge rubrics (the 3-judge evaluation)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source : MT-JailBench engine/utils/independent_judge.py (JailbreakJudge)
License: MIT License ("The MT-JailBench Authors", 2026)
File   : src/mt_jailbench_claim/_vendor/mtjb/engine/utils/independent_judge.py

Vendored byte-for-byte (sha256-pinned in _vendor/SHA256SUMS). The four rubric
prompt templates and MAX_JUDGE_RETRY are read verbatim from this file via ast
(no rubric text is reproduced in authored code). The judge parsers and the
unanimous majority-vote aggregation are reimplemented in async form over
litellm in src/mt_jailbench_claim/judge.py.

Default evaluation (shipped config 1_2_3_resource_judge + run_benchmark retro
path): judge_types = [XTeaming, ChainOfAttack, FootInTheDoor], threshold = 3
(unanimous, 3-of-3). Default judge model = openai/gpt-4o (the paper default),
overridable per factory call.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Task set (HarmBench standard behaviors)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Source : HarmBench (https://github.com/centerforaisafety/HarmBench),
         redistributed in MT-JailBench as data/harmbench_behaviors.csv
License: MIT License (HarmBench; see LICENSES/harmbench-MIT.txt)
File   : src/mt_jailbench_claim/data/harmbench_behaviors.csv

Vendored byte-for-byte (sha256-pinned). The claim filters
FunctionalCategory == "standard" (MT-JailBench data/harmbench.py::load_datasets),
yielding 159 behaviors of 320 total rows. HARMBENCH IS A DISTINCT UPSTREAM from
MT-JailBench and is attributed separately here. superred's existing `harmbench`
claim evaluates the same standard behaviors with the HarmBench Llama-2
classifier; this claim differs only in the judge.

